Status: in review. Owner: Hana Lindqvist, product lead for the clinics squad. Last updated 2026-10-21.

## Summary

This document describes online appointment booking in the Pawpoint app, so pet owners can book, move and cancel visits at any Pawpoint clinic without calling the front desk. It covers the booking rules, the appointment types that can be booked online, payments, reminders, the staff tools clinics need, and the plan to roll it out across the chain. It does not cover the clinic scheduling system itself, which the practice systems team owns.

## Background {agent=skip}

Pawpoint runs 64 veterinary clinics in four regions. Until now every appointment has been booked by phone or at the front desk. The app launched in 2023 with pet profiles, vaccination records and a clinic finder, and "book online" has been the most requested feature in every app review since then.

In spring 2025 we ran a booking pilot in three clinics (Eastgate, Millbrook and Harbourside). The pilot used a web form that sent a request to the clinic, and front desk staff confirmed each request by phone. Owners liked being able to ask for a time in the evening, but 31% of requests needed a call back because the requested time was already taken, and front desk staff said the form created more work than it saved. The pilot ended in August 2025.

The lesson we took from the pilot is that booking has to be real-time against the clinic's own schedule, or it is not worth doing. The practice systems team finished the scheduling API that makes this possible in June 2026, which is why this work is starting now.

Some history that comes up in conversations: the clinic schedule used to be kept in a separate tool per region, and the North region still had paper diaries in two clinics until 2024. All 64 clinics now use the same scheduling system, called Rota internally.

## Problem

Front desk staff spend a large part of their day on the phone. A time study in four clinics in March 2026 found that booking, moving and cancelling appointments took 38% of front desk phone time. Owners call during opening hours, which are also the busiest hours at the desk, so calls go unanswered and owners give up. At the same time about 11% of booked appointments are no-shows, and most of those owners say they forgot.

We think online booking with reminders will cut the phone load, reduce no-shows and let owners book when it suits them, often in the evening.

## Goals and non-goals

Goals for the first release:

- 35% of routine appointments booked through the app within six months of full rollout.
- No-show rate for app-booked appointments under 6%.
- Front desk phone time spent on booking down by a quarter in pilot clinics.
- No increase in double bookings or schedule errors compared to phone booking.

Non-goals. Online booking for exotic animals, birds and farm animals is not supported in the first release. These visits need a vet to check the clinic has the right equipment and staff, so they stay phone only. We are also not building a waitlist, group bookings for breeders, or home visits. Payments for treatment stay at the clinic. The app only handles the late-cancellation fee described below.

## Users

- **Pet owners** with a Pawpoint account and at least one pet profile. About 212,000 owners have the app and 148,000 were active in the last 90 days.
- **Front desk staff** who see app bookings in Rota and handle anything the app cannot.
- **Clinic managers** who set which appointment types and vets are bookable online, and can override the schedule.
- **Vets and nurses**, who mostly see the result in their day list and are not direct users of the booking feature.

An owner can only book for pets on their own account. Pets shared between two accounts (for example two people in one household) can be booked from either account, and both accounts see the appointment.

## Appointment types

The table lists the appointment types that are bookable online in the first release and how long each slot is. Slot lengths come from Rota and clinics cannot change them per booking.

| Type | Slot length | Bookable online | Notes |
|-|-|-|-|
| Consultation | 15 min | Yes | Dogs, cats and rabbits only |
| Vaccination | 10 min | Yes | Boosters and first vaccinations |
| Nurse check (weight, nails, follow-up) | 10 min | Yes | Nurse diary, not vet diary |
| Dental check | 20 min | Yes | Check only, not dental treatment |
| Post-op check | 15 min | Yes | Must be with the surgery clinic |
| Second opinion | 30 min | No | Needs records sent first |
| Surgery | varies | No | Phone only, see below |
| Euthanasia | 30 min | No | Phone only, always |

Surgery cannot be booked online. The one exception is the post-op check, which owners can book online as long as it is at the clinic that did the surgery and within 14 days of the operation date. First vaccinations for puppies and kittens younger than 8 weeks cannot be booked online either, because the clinic needs to talk to the owner about the course first. The app asks for the pet's date of birth and hides the vaccination type for pets under 8 weeks old.

Euthanasia appointments are never offered in the app. The app shows the clinic phone number instead and a short message written with the clinical team. This was a clear request from clinic staff and is not up for discussion in later releases.

## Booking rules

These rules apply to every online booking. Rota enforces some of them already. The rest the booking service has to enforce before it writes to Rota.

Owners can book from 1 hour to 60 days ahead. Bookings closer than 1 hour go through the clinic by phone, because the vet may already be running late and the desk needs to decide. The 60-day limit matches how far ahead clinics publish their rosters. Some clinics publish only 4 weeks ahead, and in that case the app shows only the slots that exist.

An owner account can hold at most 3 upcoming appointments across all clinics at any one time. The limit is per account, not per pet. Breeders and owners with many animals asked about this in the pilot, and the answer for them is to call the clinic.

When an owner picks a slot, the booking service places a hold on it in Rota while the owner fills in the details. A hold lasts 7 minutes. If the owner does not confirm in time, the hold is released and the slot goes back on the list. An account can hold only one slot at a time, so starting a new booking releases any earlier hold.

Owners can move an appointment up to 2 times. After that the app asks them to cancel and book again, which we expect to be rare. Moving keeps the same appointment number in Rota so the clinic's notes stay attached.

Cancellation is free up to 24 hours before the appointment. Inside 24 hours the late-cancellation fee applies (see Payments). There is no fee when the clinic cancels, or when the owner cancels because the pet has died, which the cancel screen asks about in a gentle way.

:::danger
> **Important:** Two confirmed appointments for the same vet must never overlap, not even when a clinic manager overrides the schedule. In the pilot, overlapping bookings in the surgery diary caused two operations to be cancelled on the day, and one animal had to be sent to another clinic.
:::

Managers can override most rules for a single booking from the staff screen, for example to book a consultation 2 hours ahead or to give an owner a fourth appointment. Overrides are logged with the manager's name. Front desk staff cannot override, except for the 1-hour rule, which any staff member can override at the desk when the owner is standing in the clinic.

Slots for each vet are shown only when Rota marks that vet as "online bookable" for that day. Locum vets are never bookable online. Clinics often hire locums at short notice, and owners asked in the pilot to see a named vet they know.

## Triage questions

Before showing consultation slots, the app asks three questions. The clinical team wrote them and owns the wording, so the text below is the current version, not a draft for product to change.

1. Is your pet having trouble breathing, bleeding heavily, or unable to stand?
2. Has your pet eaten something poisonous, such as chocolate, grapes, rat bait or medicine for people?
3. Has your pet been sick or had diarrhoea more than three times today?

A "yes" to any question stops the booking. The app shows the clinic phone number during opening hours and the out-of-hours emergency line at other times, with the text "Please call us now so we can help straight away." The owner can go back and change an answer, and we log that it happened but not the answers themselves.

Triage is skipped for vaccinations, nurse checks and post-op checks. It is asked again on every booking and is not remembered between bookings.

In the pilot about 2% of booking attempts would have hit a "yes", based on the reasons owners typed. The clinical team thinks the real number will be lower, because owners with an emergency usually phone.

## Payments and deposits

:::decision{status=superseded}
Online bookings take a deposit of €15 when the owner confirms. The deposit is taken by card through the app, held against the appointment, and taken off the bill at the clinic. If the owner does not turn up, the clinic keeps the deposit. If they cancel more than 24 hours ahead, it is refunded in full within 5 working days. We chose a deposit because the pilot showed that owners who had paid something were much less likely to miss the visit.
:::

Card details are never stored by Pawpoint. The payment provider holds the card and gives us a token. Phone wallets on iOS and Android are both accepted through the provider.

Refunds over €50 need a clinic manager to approve them. There are no other manual steps in the payment flow.

## Reminders and notifications

Owners get a confirmation as soon as a booking is made, a reminder 48 hours before the visit, and a second reminder 3 hours before. Each message has links to move or cancel.

Push notifications are the default. SMS is used only when the owner has turned on SMS reminders in the app settings and has a verified mobile number. We do not send reminders by email in the first release, because email open rates in the pilot were too low to matter, under 20%.

:::warning
> **Warning:** Reminder messages must not include the reason for the visit or any medical detail about the pet. They can include the pet's name, the clinic, the date and time, and the vet's name.
:::

Owners can turn off reminders completely. Confirmations cannot be turned off, since they are the owner's record of the booking.

Quiet hours apply to reminders. No reminder is sent between 21:00 and 08:00 in the clinic's time zone. A 3-hour reminder that would fall in quiet hours is sent at 08:00 instead, or skipped if the appointment is before 09:00.

## Staff tools

Clinic managers get a settings page in the Pawpoint staff portal where they choose which appointment types are bookable online, and which vets and nurses take online bookings. These settings take effect within 5 minutes. A clinic can switch online booking off completely, for example during a staff shortage, and then the app shows the phone number instead of slots.

Front desk staff see app bookings in Rota with an "App" label and the owner's notes. They can move or cancel app bookings like any other. When they do, the owner gets the same notification as if they had done it in the app.

Regional managers can see online booking reports for every clinic in their region, but they cannot change another clinic's settings. Only the clinic's own manager can do that, except for the head of clinical operations, who can change any clinic's settings.

## Booking flow

The diagram shows the main path for a new booking, from opening the clinic page to the confirmation.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  accTitle: Online booking flow
  participant O as Owner app
  participant B as Booking service
  participant R as Rota
  participant P as Payment provider
  O->>B: Search slots (clinic, type, date range)
  B->>R: Get free slots
  R-->>B: Slots
  B-->>O: Slots the owner may book
  O->>B: Pick slot
  B->>R: Hold slot (7 min)
  O->>B: Confirm with pet and notes
  B->>P: Save card for late-cancellation fee
  P-->>B: Token
  B->>R: Confirm appointment
  B-->>O: Confirmation and reminders scheduled
```

If Rota rejects the confirmation, for example because a manager has just blocked the slot, the booking service releases the hold, tells the owner the slot is gone, and shows the next three free slots for the same vet.

## API

The app talks to the booking service, never to Rota directly. The booking service is a new service owned by the clinics squad. These are the endpoints the app needs for the first release. All of them need a signed-in owner token, and every response uses the clinic's local time with an offset.

### Search free slots

:::api{method=GET path="/v1/clinics/{clinicId}/slots"}
`GET /v1/clinics/{clinicId}/slots`

Query parameters: `type` (an appointment type code, required), `from` and `to` (dates, at most 14 days apart), and `vetId` (optional). The response lists free slots the owner may book, already filtered by the booking rules for that owner. A search returns at most 200 slots. The app asks for a week at a time.

Rate limit: 30 searches per minute per account. Above that the service returns `429` with a `Retry-After` header.
:::

### Hold a slot

:::api{method=POST path="/v1/holds"}
`POST /v1/holds`

Body: `clinicId`, `slotId`, `petId`. Returns a hold with an `expiresAt` time 7 minutes ahead. Returns `409` if the slot was taken in the meantime, and `422` if a booking rule blocks it (the body says which rule).
:::

### Confirm a booking

:::api{method=POST path="/v1/appointments"}
`POST /v1/appointments`

```json
{
  "holdId": "hld_7Qm2c9",
  "petId": "pet_51830",
  "notes": "Limping on front left paw since Sunday",
  "reminders": { "push": true, "sms": false },
  "paymentToken": "tok_test_4f9a"
}
```

Returns `201` with the appointment, or `410` if the hold has expired. Notes are limited to 500 characters and are shown to the vet in Rota. The service writes to Rota before it replies, and if Rota does not answer within 4 seconds it returns `503` and releases the hold, so the owner never sees a booking that Rota does not have.
:::

### Move or cancel

:::api{method=PATCH path="/v1/appointments/{id}"}
`PATCH /v1/appointments/{id}` with a new `slotId` moves an appointment.
:::
:::api{method=DELETE path="/v1/appointments/{id}"}
`DELETE /v1/appointments/{id}` cancels it. Both return the fee, if any, before it is charged, and the app asks the owner to confirm when there is one.
:::

There is no endpoint to list another owner's appointments, and there will not be one. Staff use Rota for that.

## Non-functional requirements

- Slot search returns in under 800 ms at the 95th percentile, measured at the booking service. Rota calls are most of that, so we cache free slots for 30 seconds per clinic and appointment type.
- The booking service is available 99.5% of the time in clinic opening hours, measured monthly. Outside opening hours we accept short maintenance windows.
- Peak load is about 40 bookings a second, on Monday mornings after a bank holiday. The service is tested at twice that.
- Holds and confirmations are never served from the cache. Only search is.
- Personal data stays in the EU region. The booking service keeps the owner's notes for 18 months after the appointment, then deletes them. Rota keeps its own copy under the clinical records policy, which is not changed by this work.
- The booking screens meet the same accessibility level as the rest of the app, and work with text scaled to 200%.

## Security and privacy

The booking service only accepts owner tokens issued by the Pawpoint account service, and staff actions come through the staff portal with the staff member's own login. There are no shared clinic logins in the booking flow. Every write to Rota carries the identity of the person behind it, owner or staff, so the clinic can see who booked, moved or cancelled.

The owner's notes to the vet are the most sensitive thing the booking service handles. They are stored encrypted, shown only to clinic staff in Rota and to the owner in the app, and never sent to analytics, logs or support tools. Log lines carry the appointment number and clinic code, never the pet's name or the notes.

The privacy team reviewed the design on 2026-09-24 and asked for two changes, both now in this document: reminders carry no medical detail, and support agents cannot see notes. The data protection impact assessment is in the privacy team's register under booking-2026.

An owner who asks for a copy of their data gets their bookings, fees and notes from the booking service, along with what the rest of the app holds. Clinical records in Rota are handled by the clinic under its own process and are not part of the app export.

## Analytics

We track these events, all without free text from notes:

| Event | When | Properties |
|-|-|-|
| `booking_search` | Slot search returns | clinic, type, days ahead, results count |
| `booking_hold` | Hold placed | clinic, type, vet bookable flag |
| `booking_confirmed` | Rota confirms | clinic, type, days ahead, reminders chosen |
| `booking_hold_expired` | Hold times out | clinic, type, seconds on details screen |
| `booking_moved` | Move succeeds | clinic, move count |
| `booking_cancelled` | Cancel succeeds | clinic, hours before visit, fee charged |

The analytics team owns the dashboards. We need a weekly report per clinic during rollout, with app bookings, no-shows and late cancellations side by side with phone bookings from Rota.

## Edge cases

**Clinic closes for the day.** When a clinic closes unexpectedly (a flood, a power cut, a staff shortage), the manager closes the day in Rota. Every app booking on that day gets a message with a link to rebook, and the late-cancellation rules do not apply. The booking service does not try to move appointments to another clinic by itself. Owners choose.

**Vet off sick.** When a vet is marked absent, the clinic decides whether to move their appointments to another vet or cancel them. The app does not offer the owner a choice of vet in this case. The owner gets a message saying which vet they will now see, and can cancel without a fee if they would rather wait.

**Daylight saving.** All four regions are in the same time zone. Slots are stored in local time with an offset, and the app shows local time without the offset. Reminders are scheduled from the stored time, so the change of clocks in March and October does not move them.

**Pet profile changes.** If an owner deletes a pet from their account, upcoming appointments for that pet are cancelled without a fee and the clinic is told. If a pet is moved to another account, its upcoming appointments move with it.

**Account deletion.** An owner who deletes their account has their upcoming app appointments cancelled. The clinic keeps the visit history in Rota under the clinical records policy. The booking service keeps only what the finance team needs for fees already charged.

**Two owners book the same pet.** When a pet is shared between two accounts and both owners book it into the same type of appointment within 7 days, the second owner sees a notice that the pet already has an appointment and is asked to confirm. We do not block it, because two visits a few days apart can be right.

**Owner arrives late.** This is a clinic matter. The app does not mark lateness and does not charge for it.

## Support

The customer support team answers app questions by chat and email from 08:00 to 20:00 on weekdays and 09:00 to 17:00 at weekends. They can see an owner's app bookings, holds and fees in the support console, but they cannot create, move or cancel a booking. Anything that changes the schedule goes to the clinic, because only the clinic can see the vet's day.

Support can refund a late-cancellation fee of up to €20 once per owner per year without asking anyone. Any further refund for the same owner goes to the clinic manager. Support agents are not allowed to see the owner's notes to the vet, and the console hides them.

We expect most support contacts in the first weeks to be about slots that owners cannot see. The usual reasons are that the vet is not online bookable that day, the clinic publishes its roster only 4 weeks ahead, or the owner already has 3 upcoming appointments. The support console shows which of these applies, so agents do not have to guess.

Clinic staff with a problem in Rota or the staff portal contact the practice systems service desk, not customer support. The service desk has an on-call engineer for the booking service during clinic opening hours, and a response target of 30 minutes for anything that stops a clinic from taking bookings.

## Measuring success

We will judge the first release on the goals above, measured from the date each clinic switches on. The numbers come from Rota and the analytics events, and the analytics team signs them off.

| Measure | Baseline | Target | When |
|-|-|-|-|
| Share of routine appointments booked in the app | 0% | 35% | 6 months after the chain step |
| No-show rate, app bookings | 11% (all bookings, 2025) | under 6% | From the pilot onward |
| Front desk phone time on booking | 38% of phone time | down by 25% | Pilot clinics, end of pilot |
| Schedule errors per 1,000 bookings | 1.9 (phone, 2026 Q2) | no higher | Each rollout step |
| Owner satisfaction with booking | not measured | 4.3 of 5 or better | In-app survey after the visit |

A clinic whose numbers look wrong is investigated before its numbers are counted. In the pilot one clinic's no-show rate doubled for a month because the front desk was marking arrivals in a different screen, which Rota counted as no-shows.

The in-app survey asks one question after the visit and is shown to at most 1 in 5 owners, so owners do not get it after every visit.

## Rollout

The rollout goes in three steps, with a review between each.

1. **Pilot, 6 clinics, 4 weeks.** The three clinics from the 2025 pilot plus Northfield, Ashby Road and Quayside. Consultations and vaccinations only.
2. **Region, 18 clinics, 6 weeks.** All clinics in the South region, all bookable types.
3. **Chain, all 64 clinics.** In two waves of about 20 clinics a week, after the regional review.

Each step goes ahead only if the schedule error rate for app bookings is no higher than for phone bookings, and front desk staff in the step's clinics have had the 45-minute training session. A clinic can join late if its manager asks. No clinic is switched on without its manager's agreement.

The app release that carries booking is 7.4. Booking stays behind a feature flag per clinic, so turning a clinic off does not need an app release.

## Risks

:::risk{impact=high likelihood=medium}
Risk: clinics do not keep vet availability in Rota up to date, so the app offers slots that the clinic cannot honour. Impact high, likelihood medium. In the pilot two of the three clinics kept Rota current, one did not. Mitigation: the rollout step review looks at moved and cancelled-by-clinic bookings per clinic, and clinics above 5% get a visit from the regional practice lead.
:::

:::risk{impact=high likelihood=low}
Risk: owners book consultations for problems that need an urgent visit and wait days for the slot. Impact high, likelihood low. Mitigation: the booking screen asks three short triage questions written by the clinical team, and if any answer suggests an emergency the app shows the clinic phone number and the out-of-hours line instead of slots.
:::

:::risk{impact=low likelihood=medium}
Risk: SMS costs grow faster than planned. Impact low, likelihood medium. Each SMS costs about €0.04 and we budgeted for 60% of owners choosing push only.
:::

## Decision log

Decisions are listed oldest first. Each one says what was decided, when, and by whom.

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-07-30}
**D1. Real-time booking against Rota.** Accepted 2026-07-30 by Hana Lindqvist and Owen Price (practice systems). Online bookings go straight into Rota through the scheduling API, and the owner gets an instant confirmation. We do not repeat the 2025 request-form model, where staff confirmed each request by phone.
:::

:::decision{status=rejected date=2026-08-12}
**D2. Buy a third-party booking widget.** Rejected 2026-08-12. Two vendors offered booking pages that clinics could embed. Both needed their own copy of the vet schedule, synced every 15 minutes, which would bring back the double-booking problem from the pilot. Neither could show bookings inside the Pawpoint app without a web view, and neither supported shared pets. We will build the booking service ourselves.
:::

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-10-14}
**D3. Late-cancellation fee instead of a deposit.** Accepted 2026-10-14 by Hana Lindqvist, Grace Adeyemi (finance) and Dr Paul Moreau (clinical director). This replaces the deposit described under Payments and deposits, which was agreed in September. No money is taken at booking. Instead the app saves the owner's card through the payment provider when they book, and charges a fee of €20 if the owner cancels less than 24 hours ahead or does not turn up. The clinic manager can waive the fee from the staff portal, and it is never charged for a post-op check. Finance and the clinical team preferred this because a deposit put off owners on low incomes in user testing, and refunding deposits at the clinic desk was a lot of manual work.
:::

:::decision{status=proposed date=2026-10-20}
**D4. Show consultation prices in the app.** Proposed 2026-10-20 by the marketing team. Prices differ between regions and clinics change them twice a year. Product has not agreed to this yet and it is not in scope for 7.4.
:::

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-09-03}
**D5. Owners choose a named vet or "first available".** Accepted 2026-09-03. The search shows slots per vet by default, with a "first available" toggle that merges all bookable vets at the clinic. This was the most common request from pilot owners.
:::

## Open questions

:::question
**Multi-pet appointments.** Owners with two or more pets often want them seen in one visit, for example for boosters. Rota has no concept of one appointment for several pets, and practice systems have not said whether they will add one. Undecided. Until then, each pet needs its own appointment, and the app suggests back-to-back slots with the same vet when an owner books a second pet on the same day. The clinic can treat them as one visit at the desk.
:::

:::question
**Do nurse checks count toward the 3-appointment limit?** Clinics in the South region asked for nurse checks to be left out of the limit, since they are short and often booked in a series. Not decided yet. Until we decide, nurse checks count toward the limit like any other appointment, and managers can override as for any booking.
:::

:::question
**Second language.** Two clinics in the North region asked for the booking screens in a second language. The rest of the app is English only. This is open, with no date for a decision, and the booking screens are English only in 7.4.
:::

## Content and tone

Booking copy is short and plain, and it never uses clinical terms the owner did not type themselves. Error messages say what happened and what the owner can do next, in that order. The clinical team reviews every message that mentions a pet's health, including the triage questions and the cancel screen. The copy deck is kept by the content designer, Ines Faria, and changes to it go through her.

## Appendix: meeting notes {agent=skip}

These notes are kept for the people who were there and for context. They are not requirements.

### 2026-09-17, payments review

Present: Hana, Grace, Owen, Dr Moreau, Ines, and two clinic managers (Eastgate and Quayside). Grace walked through the deposit flow. The clinic managers were worried about refunds at the desk, because the desk system cannot refund a card payment made in the app, so every refund would need a call to finance. Dr Moreau raised the point that some owners book the cheapest slots because money is tight, and a deposit could stop them booking at all. We agreed to run the deposit screen past ten owners in the next round of user testing before building it. Ines to write two versions of the screen.

### 2026-10-14, decision on deposits

Present: Hana, Grace, Dr Moreau, Ines. User testing results: four of ten owners said they would phone instead of booking in the app if they had to pay a deposit, and two of those four said cost was the reason. The saved-card fee tested better. Grace confirmed the payment provider supports saving a card without a charge. We dropped the deposit and agreed the fee, see D3 in the decision log. Hana to update the PRD, which was done on 2026-10-21. There was a short discussion about charging the fee for no-shows at post-op checks, and Dr Moreau was firm that we should not, since owners sometimes miss them because the pet is doing well and they forget.

### 2026-10-20, rollout planning

Present: Hana, Owen, the South region practice lead, and three clinic managers. The South region wants to go second because its clinics already keep Rota up to date. Owen said the scheduling API can take the load of all 64 clinics but asked for a two-week gap before the chain step, to watch error rates. Nobody objected to the pilot clinics. Training sessions are booked for the first week of the pilot.
